_refine_special_details
;Since the unitary structure factors displayed centric statistics, space
group P-1 was assumed from the outset. However, all attempts to solve the
structure was unsuccessful. A solution in space group P1 was tried by
simply fixing the position of one of the Pb atoms randomly and locating the
remaining Pb and I atoms in difference maps. This method yielded a
chemically meaningful arrangement of Pb and I atoms.  It was noted that
these atoms had a centrosymmetric arrangement, so the unique set was
translated to conform to the inversion center in space group P1bar and all
further refinement was performed in the centrosymmetric space group.
Remaining nonhydrogen atoms were located in subsequent difference Fourier
synthesis. Hydrogens could not be located in difference maps, and no
attempt was made to include them in ideal positions. One of the THF
molecules was found to be significantly disordered over two distinct
positions, with population factors of 70%:30% for the O3:O3A orientations.
The major orientation was refined using mild distant constraints and the
minor orientation was refined as a rigid body based on the idealized best
model found while refining the individual atoms with mild constraints. No
unusually high correlations were noted between any of the variables in the
last cycle of full least squares refinement, and the final difference map
showed a maximum peak of 2.3 e / \%A^3^ , located near Pb.
;

